## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

### Summary

| Severity | Count |
|----------|-------|
| **Critical** | 5 |
| **High** | 1 |
| **Medium** | 2 |

This is a textbook **coverage-touching** test suite. Every public method is called, driving line coverage to ~95%, but most tests verify *nothing*. Bugs in `UpdatePrice`, `ApplyBulkDiscount`, `RemoveProduct`, `ImportFromCsv`, and `GetProductCount` would never be caught.

---

### Critical Findings

#### 1. Coverage-Touching Pattern (Systemic)

The entire class methodically calls each public member with no meaningful assertions. 5 of 10 tests have **zero assertions**; the other 5 only assert `IsNotNull` — which is trivially true for non-nullable list/string returns.

---

#### 2. No Assertions — `AddProduct_Works` (line 17)

Calls `AddProduct` but never verifies the product was persisted.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_PersistsProduct()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);

    var retrieved = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual("Widget", retrieved?.Name);
    Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, retrieved?.Price);
}
```

---

#### 3. No Assertions — `UpdatePrice_Works` (line 31)

Calls `UpdatePrice` but never checks the price changed. A broken implementation would pass.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void UpdatePrice_ChangesPrice()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);

    var updated = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
    Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, updated!.Price);
}
```

---

#### 4. No Assertions — `RemoveProduct_Works` (line 38)

Never verifies the product was actually deleted.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void RemoveProduct_DeletesProduct()
{
    _catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
    _catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");

    Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
}
```

---

#### 5. No Assertions — `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (line 68)

Applies a 10% discount to two products but never checks prices changed. This is the highest-risk gap — a bulk discount bug would silently pass.

**Fix:**
```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ApplyBulkDiscount_ReducesPrices()
{
